A column's type narrowed, TEXT to INTEGER, in a STRICT table: a value that
is not an integer cannot be copied. SQLite says so; nothing changes.

== before
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, price TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO dish VALUES (1, '95'), (2, 'cheap');

== after
CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, price INTEGER NOT NULL) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `dish`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "dish" RENAME TO "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE dish (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, price INTEGER NOT NULL) STRICT
  table `dish`: its rows, by id, price
    INSERT INTO "dish" ("id", "price") SELECT "id", "price" FROM "roux_old_dish"
  table `dish`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_dish"

== error
migrating test.db: table `dish`: its rows, by id, price: cannot store TEXT value in INTEGER column dish.price
